{"id":1052208,"date":"2026-10-06T11:30:07","date_gmt":"2026-10-06T15:30:07","guid":{"rendered":"https://www.nasa.gov/?p=1052208"},"modified":"2026-10-06T11:30:09","modified_gmt":"2026-10-06T15:30:09","slug":"nasas-curiosity-rover-catches-stunning-martian-dawn","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-science-laboratory/curiosity-rover/nasas-curiosity-rover-catches-stunning-martian-dawn/","title":{"rendered":"NASA’s Curiosity Rover Catches Stunning Martian Dawn"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"\" class=\"padding-top-5 padding-bottom-3 width-full maxw-full hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-article-intro\"><div class=\"width-full maxw-full article-header\"><div class=\"margin-bottom-2 width-full maxw-full\"><p class=\"label carbon-60 margin-0 margin-bottom-3 padding-0\">3 min read</p><h1 class=\"display-48 margin-bottom-2\">NASA’s Curiosity Rover Catches Stunning Martian Dawn</h1></div></div></div>\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1837\" height=\"1006\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?w=1837\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A stark, grayish-tan Martian landscape features jagged rock formations and rolling mountain ridges beneath a pale, hazy sky.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png 1837w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=300,164 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=768,421 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=1024,561 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=1536,841 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=400,219 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=600,329 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=900,493 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/1-pia26728-fig-a-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs.png?resize=1200,657 1200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1837px) 100vw, 1837px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">The captivating shapes of wind-carved crags called yardangs are revealed in this zoomed-in crop of a broader panorama captured by NASA’s Curiosity Mars rover on Aug. 11, 2026. Scientists are eager to learn more about how the yardang layer formed.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A newly released panorama captured by NASA’s Curiosity rover offers the most detailed view yet of distant, wind-carved Martian cliffs, highlighting features the mission’s scientists have long been waiting to see up close. The scene was snapped at 8:30 a.m. local Mars time, showing striking blue hues in the foreground as bright morning light illuminates crags known as yardangs on the horizon. &nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The panorama was captured by Curiosity’s <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity/science-instruments/#h-mastcam\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Mastcam</a> on Aug. 11, the 4,982nd Martian day, or sol, of the mission, and comprises six individual images that were stitched together after being sent to Earth. Unlike most of Curiosity’s Mastcam images, this one was processed without white balancing, preserving the early morning appearance.  </p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-none \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"382\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A broad, panoramic view of a stark, grayish-tan Martian landscape features jagged rock formations and rolling mountain ridges beneath a hazy sky. Features in the front are darker while distant rock formations are washed in pale light.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg 6737w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=300,56 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=768,143 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=1024,191 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=1536,287 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=2048,382 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=400,75 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=600,112 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=900,168 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=1200,224 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/pia26728-curiosity-captures-dawn-breaking-on-wind-carved-cliffs-.jpg?resize=2000,373 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">The six shots that make up this panorama, captured by NASA’s Curiosity on Aug. 11, were stitched together after being sent back to Earth. Unlike most images from the rover’s Mastcam, this one was processed without the usual white balancing — an artistic choice that preserves the early morning appearance.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The yardang layer extends roughly 10 miles (16 kilometers) across the northwestern reaches of Mount Sharp, a 3-mile-tall (5-kilometer-tall) mountain that Curiosity has been ascending since 2014. In fact, the rover recently passed another milestone in its ascent, reaching <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/curiosity-reaches-1-kilometer-elevation-gain/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">0.6 miles (1 kilometer) of elevation</a> — the most ever climbed on Mars. As it draws ever-closer to the yardangs, mission scientists expect to get even more amazing imagery — and, they hope, answers to how these cliffs were created. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mount Sharp is made up of layers, each recording a distinct period of Mars’ history. By studying them, scientists have been able to learn more about <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-curiosity-finds-surprise-clues-to-mars-watery-past/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">lakes and streams</a> that covered this part of the Red Planet billions of years ago. Eventually, the water dried up and left salty minerals behind. After new material stopped settling on the mountain, some of it may have been stripped away by wind, giving rise to the yardangs. </p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-image-carousel grid-container grid-container-block padding-top-4 padding-bottom-4 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-image-carousel\">\t\t<div id=\"carousel-wrapper-carousel-6acaf84e8ed65\" class=\"hds-carousel-wrapper\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"image-carousel-slider margin-0\" id=\"image-carousel-slider-carousel-6acaf84e8ed65\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-client-id=\"carousel-6acaf84e8ed65\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-variation=\"carousel\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-autoplay=\"\" \n\t\t\t\tdata-autoplay-speed=\"2000\" \n\t\t\t\tdata-play-pause=\"\" \n\t\t\t\tdata-transition-type=\"slide\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-progress=\"\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-progress-labels=\"\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-start-label=\"Start\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-end-label=\"End\"\n\t\t\t\tdata-labels-initialized=\"true\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-block width-full\" data-label=\"\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<figure class=\"margin-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-cover-wrapper hds-image-carousel-slide margin-bottom-2\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?w=1024\" class=\"attachment-large size-large\" alt=\"A 3D topographical map shows a large Martian crater with a prominent central mountain peak, encircled by a black oval highlighting a specific region near the base of the mound.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg 2560w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=300,169 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=768,432 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=1024,576 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=1536,864 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=2048,1152 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=400,225 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=600,338 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=900,506 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=1200,675 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2a-pia15101orig-yardang-annotation-1.jpg?resize=2000,1125 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">This artist’s concept shows Mount Sharp, the 3-mile-tall (5-kilometer-tall) mountain that NASA’s Curiosity has been ascending since 2014. The location of the yardang layer, which the rover recently captured from a distance, is indicated by an ellipse annotation on the mountain’s northwestern shoulder.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/JPL-Caltech/ESA/DLR/FU Berlin/MSSS</div></figcaption></div>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</figure>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-block width-full\" data-label=\"\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<figure class=\"margin-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-cover-wrapper hds-image-carousel-slide margin-bottom-2\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"371\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?w=1024\" class=\"attachment-large size-large\" alt=\"A wide panoramic view of a rocky, reddish-brown Martian valley framed by rugged hills under a hazy sky, with a portion of NASA’s Curiosity Mars visible on the far right.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg 11027w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=300,109 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=768,278 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=1024,371 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=1536,556 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=2048,742 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=400,145 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=600,217 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=900,326 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=1200,435 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/e2b-pia26729-curiosity-looks-back-after-reaching-altitude-milestone.jpg?resize=2000,724 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" /></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">NASA’s Curiosity Mars rover captured this view looking back at the floor of Gale Crater on Sept. 5, 2026. Just nine days prior, the rover had marked 1 kilometer of elevation gain above the crater floor since beginning its ascent of Mount Sharp in 2014.</div><div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS</div></figcaption></div>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</figure>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"hds-carousel-nav display-flex margin-left-auto margin-right-0\" data-carousel-id=\"image-carousel-slider-carousel-6acaf84e8ed65\">\n\t\t\t\t<button class=\"hds-carousel-nav-arrow hds-carousel-arrow-prev\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<svg version=\"1.1\" x=\"0px\" y=\"0px\" width=\"9px\" height=\"9px\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\"><path class=\"st0\" d=\"M3.5,4.5l3.7-3.6L6.3,0L1.8,4.5L6.3,9l0.9-0.9L3.5,4.5z\"></path></svg>\n\t\t\t\t</button>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<button class=\"hds-carousel-nav-arrow hds-carousel-arrow-next margin-right-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<svg version=\"1.1\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0px\" y=\"0px\" width=\"9px\" height=\"9px\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\"><path class=\"st0\" d=\"M5.5,4.5L1.8,8.1L2.7,9l4.5-4.5L2.7,0L1.8,0.9L5.5,4.5z\"></path></svg>\n\t\t\t\t</button>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">“The yardang layer looks out of place. It’s the wrong color, the layers tilt at an odd angle, and it almost appears plastered on,” said Ashwin Vasavada, Curiosity project scientist of NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Southern California. “But that’s what makes it exciting to reach. No one is sure exactly what created this layer, but one idea is it may be ash deposited by ancient volcanic eruptions.”&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Curiosity is in the second year of its fifth extended mission, which started with an exploration of the <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/mars-science-laboratory/curiosity-rover/nasas-curiosity-rover-sees-martian-spiderwebs-up-close/\">spiderweb-like boxwork ridges</a>. The rover will spend most of the next year or so driving through layers enriched with sulfates and carbonates, both signs of ancient drying on the surface. Sometime in 2027, scientists hope to reach the base of the yardangs, where Curiosity will be able to use its robotic arm to collect one-of-a-kind data on these mysterious features. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>More about Curiosity</strong>&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Curiosity was built by NASA JPL, which is managed by Caltech in Pasadena, California. NASA JPL leads the mission on behalf of the agency’s Science Mission Directorate in Washington as part of its Mars Exploration Program portfolio. Malin Space Science Systems in San Diego built and operates Mastcam.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To learn more about Curiosity, visit:</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"><strong>https://science.nasa.gov/mission/msl-curiosity</strong></a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>News Media Contacts</strong></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Andrew Good<br>Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Pasadena, Calif.<br>818-393-2433<br><a href=\"mailto:andrew.c.good@jpl.nasa.gov\">andrew.c.good@jpl.nasa.gov</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Karen Fox / Alana Johnson<br>NASA Headquarters, Washington<br>240-285-5155 / 202-672-4780<br><a href=\"mailto:karen.c.fox@nasa.gov\">karen.c.fox@nasa.gov</a> / <a href=\"mailto:alana.r.johnson@nasa.gov\">alana.r.johnson@nasa.gov</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">2026-067</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"nasa-gb-align-full width-full maxw-full padding-x-3 padding-y-0 nasa_template_article_a hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-credits-and-details\">\n<section class=\"padding-x-0 padding-top-5 padding-bottom-2 desktop:padding-top-7 desktop:padding-bottom-9\">\n\t<div class=\"grid-row grid-container maxw-widescreen padding-0\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-2 padding-right-4 margin-bottom-5 desktop:margin-bottom-0\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"padding-top-3 border-top-1px border-color-carbon-black\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"margin-bottom-2\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<h2 class=\"heading-14\">Share</h2>\n\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"padding-bottom-2\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<ul class=\"social-icons social-icons-round\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<li class=\"social-icon social-icon-x\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://x.co